# internal/features/

One directory per vertical slice. A feature owns its handlers, use cases,
SQL, domain rules, and tests: `domain.go`, `usecase.go`, `handler.go`,
`sqlc.yaml`, `queries/`, `store/`, and its own tests. Its frontend half is
`frontend/src/routes/(app)/<plural>/` plus
`frontend/src/lib/features/<feature>/`.

Four rules hold across every feature:

- **The use case owns the transaction boundary.** Handlers do transport
  work; the `store` package runs queries and never begins a business
  transaction of its own. The use case calls
  `database.Transactor.Within`, propagates its callback context, and passes
  the callback's `pgx.Tx` to `store.New(tx)`. Nested runners are rejected.
- **A feature depends on another feature only through an interface the
  consumer declares**, satisfied by the other feature's use case. A feature
  never imports another feature's `store` package.
- **SQL source and configuration are feature-owned.** `queries/` and
  `sqlc.yaml` are application-owned; `store/` is generated by
  sqlc and never hand-edited. The config points to
  `../../../db/migrations` rather than duplicating the schema.
- **Direct pgx is a reviewed exception, not a second repository style.** When
  sqlc cannot model a PostgreSQL protocol operation, keep a narrowly named
  adapter in the owning feature. It receives the use case's callback `pgx.Tx`,
  exposes only required tracer-covered methods, and uses static SQL and
  identifiers with bound values. See the compile-checked
  `internal/platform/database/directpgx_test.go` example.

Run `make sqlc-compile` to statically validate every feature's SQL,
`make sqlc-vet` to enforce bounded mutation and output-confinement rules, and
`make sqlc-generate` to rebuild every `store/`, in stable feature path order.
All three commands are honest no-ops while this directory has no feature-local
`sqlc.yaml`. Each config must emit exactly to its own `store/` and carry the
canonical `nise-no-unbounded-delete`, `nise-no-unbounded-update`, and
`nise-no-truncate` CEL rules documented in the project README.

Named reference-flow tests attach `database.WithQueryBudget` to the use-case
context and assert the exact count plus the maximum. A query count means one
pgx round trip: one ordinary query/exec, one copy, or one whole batch. Changing
an established count needs an explanation; batch tests also bound the queued
statement count because batching hides statements, not PostgreSQL work.

`auth/` is the first feature and the worked example of every rule above: its
queries are hand-written SQL in `queries/`, its `store/` is complete sqlc
output, and `sessions.go` and `accounts.go` are use cases that own their
transaction boundaries. Authorization and the audit log join it as the
authentication milestone continues, and custom features are written by
`nise generate feature` from the generation milestone onward. Nothing in the
layout moves when they land.

Neither `queries/` nor `store/` carries an ownership header comment, and both
declare ownership by path instead. A comment in a query file is not inert —
sqlc copies it into the generated Go doc comment — so a header saying "Nise
will not overwrite it" placed there would appear inside a tool-owned file and
contradict that file's own marker. `queries/` is yours; `store/` belongs to
sqlc and is replaced wholesale.
